The comment attached to that block in `zoneC()` (`packages/core/src/assemble/assembler.ts`) reads:

```
// The descendant map goes LAST: it grows with every edit, and churn ahead
// of the append-only event stream voids the events' cache (see
// renderActiveMap). Callers that cache Zone C must keep it after their
// moving breakpoint.
```

So the requirement it states: **callers that cache Zone C must keep the `C:map` block after their moving breakpoint** — i.e., excluded from the cached span.

The reasoning behind it: the descendant map grows on every file edit, so unlike the event blocks (append-only within a phase), its text churns turn over turn. If it sat ahead of the cache breakpoint, that churn would no longer be a byte-prefix match against the previously cached span, voiding the cache of everything before it — the same volatile-bit law measured live as FM-7 (iter8-rep1: cacheRead pinned at the A+B prefix while cacheWrite re-billed the whole zone during edit turns). That's why `toZoneCCachedRequest` in `eval/src/loop.ts` filters `C:map:` blocks out of the cached sequence and folds them, unmarked, into the trailing message with the tail.

The matching comment on `renderActiveMap` in `format.ts` says the same thing from the renderer's side: seq ranges are fine *there* because "this block is expected to change and is never cached."

