`['parseThing']`.

In that test ("stores the tree-sitter spans, not the model's…"), the stub model replies with invented metadata — a file span at `src/a.ts:900-901` with symbol `'ghost'`, plus `symbols: ['ghost']`. The assertions then verify the tree overrides the model on the D9-owned fields:

```js
expect(summary.meta.files).toEqual([A_SPAN]);
expect(summary.meta.symbols).toEqual(['parseThing']);
// Prose-side fields still come from the model — it is the only source for them.
expect(summary.meta.node_ids).toEqual([fx.p1, fx.f1]);
```

So `'ghost'` never survives: `symbols` is replaced wholesale with the tree-sitter-derived symbol from the fixture (`'parseThing'`), just as `files` is replaced with the real span `A_SPAN`. The point of the test is that the model only owns the prose-side fields (`text`, `tests`, `decisions`, `open_questions`, `artifacts`) — anything the tree can verify itself (spans, symbols, paths) is authoritative from the tree, which is what keeps §9's rehydration pointers trustworthy.
